//RS impl FileIdentity {
//RS     /// A regular file's identity includes its length and mtime: its bytes were
//RS     /// read from the opened handle, so drift in either means the snapshot no
//RS     /// longer describes what was read.
//RS     ///
//RS     /// A directory's length and mtime are *not* its identity. Both change
//RS     /// whenever any entry is created or removed inside it, which is exactly
//RS     /// what legitimate writers do below an authority root: the running command
//RS     /// creating `build/` or `.jet/perf/reports/`, a sibling process working in
//RS     /// a shared parent directory. A durability check that cannot tell this
//RS     /// process from an attacker is not a durability check, so a directory is
//RS     /// identified by the object itself — kind plus device and inode.
//RS     ///
//RS     /// That keeps the attack this resolver exists to stop. [`AuthorityResolver::open`]
//RS     /// compares an identity taken by path against one taken from the
//RS     /// `O_NOFOLLOW` handle; a path swapped between those two observations
//RS     /// yields a different inode and is still refused. What is lost is only a
//RS     /// "the entry list moved" signal that never was a guarantee — mtime has
//RS     /// coarse granularity and misses replace-in-place entirely. A caller that
//RS     /// genuinely needs it must compare entry sets explicitly.
//RS     ///
//RS     /// Where no stable device/inode is available the content fields stay in
